# Track

`track` is the planning command surface for Holly. It turns fuzzy requirements
into a clean, dependency-aware epic whose children can be delivered one at a
time or in parallel. It owns **what** to build; the `implement` and
`orchestrate` commands own **how** it gets built.

This skill is the durable methodology. The executable step sequence lives in
`workflows/track.yaml` and is run via the [use-workflow](../use-workflow/SKILL.md)
executor contract. Tracker-specific CLI verbs (create epic, add child, set
dependency) are adapter-flavoured — get the concrete commands from the
[issue-tracker](../issue-tracker/SKILL.md) skill and its installed adapter
addendum.

## When to use

- A new feature, fix, or chunk of work needs to be planned before coding.
- An existing epic needs to be split into children, re-ordered, or re-scoped.
- The user hands you a user story or raw requirements with no issues yet.

If a concrete, single, already-scoped issue exists and you just need to build
it, skip `track` and go straight to `implement`.

## The four phases

1. **Capture & clarify.** Parse the request for task description, user story,
   acceptance criteria, constraints, referenced files/systems, and any existing
   epic ID. If an epic ID was given, load it and check for existing children
   before adding more. If the requirements are ambiguous, ask targeted
   questions about the why, the who/what, what "done" looks like, and known
   constraints. Do not decompose until the requirements are clear enough to
   turn into concrete tasks.

2. **Scope against the codebase.** Search only as much as needed to identify
   affected files, modules, and systems, the blast radius, risks, and open
   questions. Start narrow; broaden only when scope is unclear.

3. **Decompose into children.** Break the work into discrete, independently
   deliverable issues under one epic. Each child gets a clear title,
   description, acceptance criteria, priority, and type. Establish dependencies
   between children so the tracker's ready-work view surfaces them in the right
   order.

4. **Label for delivery.** Analyse the dependency DAG and assign:
   - **wave labels** (`wave-1`, `wave-2`, …) — topological layers that show
     execution order for phased delivery.
   - **agent-path labels** (`agent-1`, `agent-2`, …) — independent dependency
     chains that partition the work so multiple agents can each take a full
     chain without blocking each other.

## Approval gate

Present the full epic structure — epic, children, dependencies, wave and
agent-path labels — for review **before creating any issues**. The user may
request re-ordering, scope changes, or different granularity. Only create the
issues once the structure is approved.

## Hand-off

Once the epic exists, deliver it with one of:

- `implement <epic-id>` — TDD delivery; epic mode iterates all unblocked
  children automatically.
- `orchestrate <epic-id>` — bounded swarm delivery across worker agents when
  the agent-path labels expose real parallelism. See the
  [orchestrate](../orchestrate/SKILL.md) skill.

## Granularity guidance

- A child should be a single coherent change a worker can finish and verify on
  its own. If a child needs its own sub-plan, it is an epic, not a child.
- Prefer dependencies over giant sequential children — they are what let waves
  and agent-paths parallelise the work.
- Do not invent children for hypothetical future work. Decompose the request in
  front of you.
